|segment[prx-was-the-author-wrong] :type probe :status fired :stage draft
  :tags [primary-sources register]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Was the agent who wrote this artifact wrong?

  |when
    Before treating any artifact in this estate as settled — a convention file, a
    README, a lesson, a banner, a commit rationale. Distinct from doubting its
    content: this doubts its *author*, which is a separate act and one that does not
    arise natively.

  |act
    Run `git log` on the artifact before relying on it. Thin revision history means
    provisional, whatever the tone. Joseph's rule of thumb, 2026-07-30: absent
    roughly four revisions over the last few weeks or more, it is more likely than
    not to be false to one degree or another.

    Then read the *shape* of the error rather than only the fact of it. An absolute
    usually marks the author's surprise reported as a property of the world. An
    over-specification usually marks an author writing to itself and forgetting the
    reader is someone else. A hedge in an odd place usually marks a specific fear
    worth naming.

  |why
    An artifact's confidence is uncorrelated with its warrant, and nothing in a
    document reports its own age or its author's state. A young single-commit file
    cited as house law is the estate's most common propagation vector — the founding
    session found a governing paragraph that was true when written and false
    twenty-three minutes later, then propagated it as an absolute eighteen hours
    after that.

  |evidence
    **Fired, 2026-07-30, five times in one afternoon**, from five unrelated
    directions: a banner's register, a parser-lesson repair, a `:see` justification,
    a founding commit's timestamp, and the probe itself. Nobody set out to ask it
    five times — that recurrence is what identified it as load-bearing
    (#prx-tally-recurrence, forward reference).

    **The move it encodes was supplied by Joseph and is not native**: *"What if the
    agent that wrote this thing was wrong in its assumptions, overly prescriptive,
    not authorized, or just outright incorrect?"* An agent reviewing an artifact
    will doubt the claim and not the claimant.

  |working-notes
    The recursion is real and must not become an escape hatch: **this corpus was
    written by agents too**, and the same test applies to it. But *the author might
    be wrong* is a reason to check, never a licence to route around a discipline
    that is inconvenient.
